  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated severe thunderstorms possible this \nafternoon, mainly south and east of the urban I-95 corridor. A \nlocalized flash flood risk today for mainly portions of Delmarva. It \nthen turns much drier and pleasant for the end of the week.\n\nA strong upper-level trough will remain across the Northeast and Mid-\nAtlantic regions today into Thursday. Some additional shortwave \nenergy will continue to slide across our area today as a result.\n\nA cold front associated with the aforementioned upper-level trough \nwill gradually arrive later today. The deep moisture continues to \nshift eastward today given the eastward progression of the upper-\nlevel trough. While a surface trough has shifted offshore overall, \nthe main cold front will arrive by later today. The forecast \nchallenge is how much convection redevelops ahead of the cold front \nthrough this afternoon. The majority of the guidance is limited with \nconvective redevelopment through today with the arrival of the cold \nfront. The combination of greater instability and shear looks to \nreside near Delmarva and especially on southward where the severe \nthunderstorm risk looks a bit higher. For our area, an isolated \nsevere thunderstorm risk is present mostly focused south and east of \nthe I-95 urban corridor this afternoon. If a few storms can become \nstrong enough, then conditions look favorable for locally strong to \ndamaging outflow driven winds.\n\nThere will be drier air gradually working in from the west and \nnorthwest through today, which will result in the dew points \ngradually lowering from north to south through this afternoon. \nDespite the deeper plume of moisture shifting eastward today \nresulting in the precipitable water values decreasing with time, it \nwill still be very near Delmarva. Therefore, some local flash \nflooding risk for that area if some deeper convection occurs and \nmoves over areas that just recently had heavy rainfall. This risk \nlooks even lower if convective coverage ends up remaining less. Once \nthe front moves away later today into tonight, it turns much drier \nand pleasant for the end of the week and right into the weekend.",
